0025 — an arc with no sweep draws nothing.

`arc-width` makes a node an arc and `arc-sweep` says how much of it. The
painter's test was `arc_width > 0 && arc_sweep != 0`, so a sweep of 0 fell
through to the box painter: the node's whole ground — a full disc on a
`rounded-full` node — on the frame a drawn-on stroke starts from, then the
arc. Films worked around it by starting every draw-on at half a degree. Now
any arc width makes the node an arc, and the arc is drawn only when its
sweep is not 0; under a sweep of 0 neither arc nor box is painted.

Class `upstream`: what an arc node paints is the declaration layer, and
every backend draws an arc from the core's RECT runs, so no backend changes.
`contracts/spec/spec.ts` states it beside `arcWidth`.

Guard: `an_arc_with_no_sweep_draws_nothing` (`engine/core/src/tests.rs`): a
`rounded-full` node with arc width 5 and sweep 0 emits no paint op; the same
node at +90 and -90 degrees, and with no arc width, still paints. Red before
the change (the sweep-0 node painted its rounded ground), green after.

Ledgers, against the same tree without this patch: no digest and no
`visibility` row moves — no composition in the ledger shows an arc at a sweep
of 0 (the films that hit it start their draw-ons at 0.5 degrees).

`git apply` skips everything above the first `diff --git`.

